      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2013. Published by Elsevier Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "AIMS",
            "content": "Post-procedural myocardial necrosis manifested by elevated cardiac troponin T (cTnT) often complicates percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI). Plasma pentraxin 3 (PTX3) levels are increased in patients with arterial inflammation and especially unstable angina pectoris (UAP). This study tested whether plasma PTX3 levels can predict post-PCI cTnT elevation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MAIN METHODS",
            "content": "We evaluated 94 consecutive patients with AP and normal pre-PCI cTnT levels who underwent PCI. Pre-PCI virtual histology-intravascular ultrasound was performed to assess culprit plaque composition. Plasma PTX3 and serum hs-CRP levels were measured pre-PCI. Patients were divided into 2 groups according to presence (Group I, n=34) or absence (Group II, n=60) of post-PCI cTnT elevation >3 × the upper limit of normal at 24h after PCI."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "KEY FINDINGS",
            "content": "Plasma PTX3 (4.06 ± 2.05 ng/ml vs 2.17 ± 1.02 ng/ml, p<0.001), serum hs-CRP levels (0.25 ± 0.03 vs 0.16 ± 0.03 mg/dl, p=0.048), plaque burden (80.9 ± 5.3 vs 75.4 ± 10.6%, p=0.047), presence of positive remodeling (59 vs 25%, p=0.034), and percent necrotic core area (19.0 ± 7.4 vs 14.0 ± 5.9%, p=0.046) were significantly higher in Group I than in Group II. Receiver-operating characteristic curve analysis showed that with a best cut-off value of 2.83 ng/ml, plasma PTX3 level (AUC 0.823) predicted post-PCI cardiac TnT elevation better than did serum hs-CRP level (AUC 0.618). Multiple logistic regression analysis showed that plasma PTX3 level was the most independent predictor of post-PCI cardiac cTnT elevation (OR: 2.65; 95% CI: 1.56-10.1; p=0.003)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "SIGNIFICANCE",
            "content": "Plasma PTX3 level may be a useful marker for predicting post-PCI cardiac cTnT elevation, which is associated with inflammatory status of culprit lesions."
          }
        ]
      },
